The daemon and the Studio plugin speak **HTTP on `localhost`**, with bodies encoded as **MessagePack**.
HTTP is forced by the Studio API (no WebSocket client); MessagePack is chosen over JSON because it is
binary, handles complex Roblox types, and has usable Luau libraries. The encoding is isolated behind
`serde` protocol types so it can be swapped (e.g. protobuf) later without a rewrite. gRPC is not pursued:
it needs HTTP/2, which `HttpService` does not support.

## Endpoints

| Method + path | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `GET /info` | Handshake: protocol/server version, project name, session id. With the project token, it re-diffs against persisted state on (re)connect; without it, it only answers. |
| `GET /patches` | Long-poll for filesystem → Studio text patches. Held open until a change is ready or a timeout elapses. Returns a cursor + ordered patches. Needs the `session` query parameter. |
| `POST /changes` | Studio → filesystem edits (a batch of upserts/deletes). |
| `POST /inventory` | The plugin's full listing of its scripts, posted once per session on attach. The daemon removes the mirrored scripts it does not name and writes the ones it lacks, so a rename made while the plugin was stopped leaves no old file. A folder renamed in Studio is followed by the scripts left under it, and the reply lists the new paths whose source the plugin then sends as upserts. Needs the `session` query parameter. The version stays at 5: the `200` body changed from empty to `{"wanted": [...]}`, and a plugin built before that discards it, so the scripts it would have sent stay under the old folder until the next attach. |
| `GET /blobs` | Long-poll for the **terrain blob** channel (daemon → Studio). Kept separate from `/patches` because blobs sync opaquely, with no diff/merge. The cursor is the ack: queued blob patches below it are dropped. Needs the `session` query parameter. |
| `POST /blobs` | Studio → daemon blob changes (terrain voxels). |
| `POST /ack` | The plugin confirms, by sequence number, which patches it actually applied. Gates the base advance. Needs the `session` query parameter. |
| `GET /heartbeat` | Liveness ping; drives the explicit connection state. |

## Access control

The daemon checks every route before its handler runs. A request is refused with `403`, or with `401` for the token, when:

- it carries an `Origin` header, which browsers add to cross-site requests;
- its `Host` is not `127.0.0.1:<port>`, `localhost:<port>` or `[::1]:<port>`, which stops web pages that
  rebind a DNS name to the loopback address;
- it lacks the `x-naht-client` header. A browser cannot send a custom header to another origin without a
  preflight, and the daemon never answers one;
- it lacks the `x-naht-token` header, or sends another value, on any route except `GET /info`; that refusal is `401`.
- the project sets `[serve] place_id` and the `x-naht-place-id` header is missing or differs;
- the project has no `[serve] place_id` and is bound to a place: a `POST` without the `x-naht-place-id` header, a
  header naming another place, or a header naming place `0` (an unpublished place) is refused. An unbound project
  accepts place `0` without recording it;
- the `x-naht-place-id` header is malformed.

These checks stop browsers and DNS rebinding. Every route except `GET /info` also needs the project token in
`x-naht-token`. On Unix the daemon creates it in `.naht/token` readable only by its owner, so other accounts on a shared
machine cannot call it. On Windows the file inherits the access rules of the `.naht` directory. The token is no defence against a process of the same account, which can read the file.
The threat model is in `SECURITY.md`.

Writes are checked against what they may touch. `POST /changes` takes a MessagePack map `{changes: [...]}`,
where each change is `{Upsert: {path, class, content}}` or `{Delete: {path}}`. It accepts only visible paths
that map to a Studio script. A batch with any refused change is refused whole: the daemon answers `400` with a
JSON body `{"refused_paths": [...]}` listing every refused path in batch order, and nothing in the batch is
written. A batch with every change accepted is answered `200` with an empty body, and a path through a symlink
is refused. A malformed body is `400`. If the valid
changes cannot be applied, the batch is answered `500` and the daemon's Studio mirror is restored to its state
before the batch. `POST /blobs` accepts only `Terrain.terrain` at the project root. A batch naming any other path
is refused whole: the daemon answers `400` with a JSON body `{"refused_paths": [...]}` naming each one, and
nothing is written.

At most two POST requests read and handle a body at once, and a third waits for a slot with no time limit of
its own. Each body is capped at 64 MiB and answered `413` when larger. The 15-second timer covers only the read
of the body, which is answered `408` when it stalls, and a body that ends early or is malformed is answered
`400`. A request head that stalls for ten seconds is dropped. By default the daemon serves at most 64 authenticated connections at once (`[limits] connections`). A connection that passes the token joins that pool and keeps its place until it closes. A connection that passes the token while the pool is full is closed when its two seconds run out. Before the token, a connection holds one of 16 seats for at most two seconds, so connections that send no token, stay silent or open with an HTTP/2 preface cannot take the seats that authenticated requests need. Connections past the 16 seats wait in arrival order for up to 20 seconds, then close, and a connection that finds 256 others already waiting closes at once. A local process that keeps about 272 tokenless connections open (16 seated and 256 waiting) makes new connections close at once, including the plugin's, and the plugin retries with its backoff. A process that holds the token can still fill the 64. A `GET /info` that waits more than two seconds for the daemon to finish a reconcile is cut at the deadline, and the plugin recovers through its reconnect backoff.

## The handshake (`GET /info`)

The handshake returns a `ServerInfo` carrying the **protocol version**, the server version, the project
name, a session id, and the optional `serve_place_id`. Two guards live here:

- **Version gate**: a protocol-version mismatch is reported instead of syncing against an incompatible
  peer. Upgrade the CLI and plugin together. See [Installation](#/docs/installation).
- **`servePlaceId` guard**: if the project sets `[serve] place_id`, every request must carry the matching
  `x-naht-place-id` header. A mismatch is refused before any source moves (see Access control).

<Callout type="important">

The `servePlaceId` guard is the cheap insurance that stops you pouring one game's source into another.
A mismatch fails on the first request. Nothing is written.

</Callout>

## Sessions

Every `GET /patches`, `GET /blobs` and `POST /ack` names the session the plugin is attached to, with the
session id it read from `GET /info` in a `session` query parameter (`?session=<id>`). The session id changes
each time the daemon starts, so the parameter tells the daemon which run a poll or an ack belongs to:

- a request without the `session` parameter is answered `400`;
- a request naming a session other than the daemon's current one is answered `409`. The plugin re-handshakes
  and resumes from the new session's cursor, and the stale request changes nothing;
- `POST /ack` is checked the same way. An ack from a previous session settles no patch, so a patch the new
  session never delivered is never confirmed by an old ack.

## Two long-poll channels

Long-polling exists because `HttpService` is request/response only, so the daemon can never push. The
plugin holds open a `GET /patches` (text) and a `GET /blobs` (terrain) request; the daemon answers the
moment a change is ready, or returns empty on timeout so the plugin can poll again.

The two channels are deliberately separate: **text patches** carry diff/merge semantics, while **blobs**
(terrain voxels) are synced opaquely: hash-compared, last-writer-wins, with a both-sides change
freezing rather than merging. See [Terrain & assets](#/docs/terrain-assets).

## Ack-gating

A Studio-bound patch advances the persisted base **only after the plugin acks its sequence** via `POST /ack`. An acked patch leaves the queue, and an ack for a sequence the daemon has since superseded confirms nothing. On
a flaky connection a half-applied batch leaves the un-acked patches un-advanced, so the next reconnect
re-diffs and resends exactly what's missing, never assuming a dropped batch landed. This is the
backbone of Naht's reconnect safety, detailed in the [Sync engine](#/docs/sync-engine).

## Versioning

The protocol carries an integer `PROTOCOL_VERSION` (currently `5`). It is part of the handshake so the
two halves can refuse to talk across an incompatible boundary instead of corrupting state.

The client headers `x-naht-client` and `x-naht-place-id` are required on every request and came with
version `2`. A plugin built for version `1` is refused on every route, and it reports that it is outdated
and stops reconnecting. Upgrade the CLI and the plugin together.

Protocol 3 changes the poll and ack routes. `GET /patches`, `GET /blobs` and `POST /ack` require the `session`
query parameter, and a stale session is answered `409`. Upgrade the CLI, the daemon and the plugin
together. A plugin built for another protocol version is refused at the handshake.

Protocol 4 adds the project token. Every route except `GET /info` requires `x-naht-token`, and a request without a
matching token is answered `401`. A plugin built for version `3` stops at the update message. Upgrade the CLI, the
daemon and the plugin together.

Protocol 5 adds the daemon proof. `GET /info?nonce=<n>` answers with `proof`, the HMAC-SHA256 of
`naht-daemon-proof-v1\n<port>\n<n>` keyed by the token, and the plugin sends the token only once that matches the
value it computes. Until then it sends nothing but `GET /info`, and with no token for the project it stops and asks
for one. A program that takes the port while `naht serve` is stopped cannot answer it, so it never sees the token and
cannot push patches into Studio. Upgrade the CLI, the daemon and the plugin together.
